course: english
lesson: 26
title: Sentences: capital letters, full stops and the four types
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sentence is a group of words that makes complete sense on its own. It starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop, a question mark or an exclamation mark. In Year 1, Jack writes: «i went to the park and i saw a dog and it was big and i played.» His teacher circles every «i», because the word I is always a capital, and shows him where to stop: «I went to the park. I saw a big dog. I played.» A trick: read it aloud, and where your voice stops for breath, a full stop probably goes.
explain: A sentence needs a verb, a doing or being word. «The dog.» is not a sentence: it is a phrase, because we don't know what the dog did. «The dog barked.» is. A sentence can be very short: Grandad in Edinburgh asks, «Aye, ken that's a sentence? "Stop." Just one word?» (ken means know in Scots). Yes: «Stop.» is a command, and the person told to stop, «you», is understood without being said.
explain: There are four types. A statement tells you something: «The bus is late.» A question asks and ends with a question mark: «Where is my hat?» A command tells someone to do something, usually starting with a bossy verb: «Close the door.» An exclamation, in school grammar, starts with What or How, has a verb and ends with an exclamation mark: «What a huge cake that is!» or «How tall you've grown!»
explain: The exclamation mark is a trap. «Stop that!» has one, but it is a command. «I love pizza!» has one, but it is a statement said with feeling. And «How are you?» starts with How but is a question, because it asks something. Lucy in Sheffield says, «Ey up, what time's tea?» (ey up is Yorkshire for hello, and tea is the evening meal in the North): whatever the accent, it asks, so it's a question.
explain: Two sentences joined only by a comma make a comma splice: «It was raining, we went inside.» Fix it with a full stop («It was raining. We went inside.») or a joining word («It was raining, so we went inside.»). Ellie ends a sentence with «!!!!!!»; in proper writing one is enough, and only where it really matters. When Ryan texts «bro come here rn» (rn means right now), that's fine in a text, but in school writing it becomes: «Come here right now.»
fact: A sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop, a question mark or an exclamation mark.
fact: The word «I» is always written with a capital letter.
fact: Names of people, places, days and months also start with capital letters.
fact: A sentence needs a verb; a group of words without one, like «The dog», is a phrase.
fact: A statement tells you something and usually ends with a full stop.
fact: A question asks something and ends with a question mark.
fact: A command tells someone to do something and often starts with a bossy (imperative) verb, like close, stop or sit.
fact: In a command, the subject «you» is understood, so «Stop.» is a complete sentence.
fact: In school grammar, an exclamation starts with What or How, contains a verb and ends with an exclamation mark: «What a mess this is!»
fact: An exclamation mark alone does not make a sentence an exclamation: «Sit down!» is a command.
fact: «How are you?» is a question, not an exclamation, because it asks something.
fact: Joining two sentences with only a comma is a comma splice; use a full stop or a joining word instead.
fact: Long chains of «and… and… and» are easier to read when split into separate sentences.
fact: Texts and chat messages often drop capitals and punctuation; school writing needs them.
exercise: Jack writes «i went to the park». What must change? | «i» must be a capital: «I went to the park.» | «park» must have a capital letter: «Park» | Nothing, small letters are fine in a story | «went» must be «gone»
exercise: Mia asks, «Isn't every sentence with ! an exclamation?» What is the answer? | No, «Sit down!» is a command | Yes, the mark decides the sentence type every time | Yes, unless it is a very short sentence | No, ! only goes after questions
exercise: Ali writes «Close the door.» and calls it a statement. What type is it? | A command | A statement | A question | An exclamation
exercise: Lucy says, «Ey up, what time's tea?» What type of sentence is it? | A question | A command, because it tells someone to make tea | An exclamation, because it starts with a greeting | A statement
exercise: Ben writes «The dog.» and thinks it's a sentence. What is missing? | A verb, saying what the dog did or was | A capital letter for «dog» | A question mark at the end | Nothing, it is a full sentence
exercise: Zara writes «It was raining, we went inside.» Which is a correct fix? | It was raining, so we went inside. | It was raining, we went inside, and we stayed there. | It was raining we went inside. | it was raining. we went inside.
exercise: Grandad asks if «Stop.» can be a sentence. What is true? | Yes, it's a command, with «you» understood | No, a sentence needs at least three words in it | No, one word is always a phrase | Yes, but only if it has an exclamation mark
exercise: Ellie ends a sentence with «!!!!!!». What is the better choice in writing? | One exclamation mark, only where it matters | Six marks, because more is more exciting | No punctuation at all | A full stop, then five more marks
exercise: Max says «How are you?» is an exclamation because it starts with How. Is he right? | No, it asks something, so it's a question | Yes, all sentences starting with How are exclamations | Yes, but only when it is said loudly | No, it is a command
exercise: Ryan texts «bro come here rn». How would it look as a full sentence in school writing? | Come here right now. | bro come here rn. | Bro, come here rn, like right now | come here right now
exercise: «Add the missing punctuation: where is my hat» | Where is my hat? | where is my hat? | Where is my hat. | Where, is my hat!
exercise: Which is an exclamation in school grammar? | What a huge cake that is! | Eat your huge cake right now! | The cake is huge! | Is the cake huge?
exercise: Which is a statement? | The bus is late. | Is the bus late? | Run for the bus! | What a long wait this is!
exercise: What does «ken» mean in Scots? | Know | Can | Kind | Again
exercise: Which group of words is a sentence? | The cat sat on the mat. | The big black cat. | On the mat by the fire. | Under the old kitchen table.
exercise: Which word in this sentence needs a capital letter: «on monday we saw the sea.» | On and Monday | Only on, because it starts the sentence | Monday, sea | Saw and sea
exercise: What is a comma splice? | Two sentences joined only by a comma | A comma used in a list of things | A sentence with no commas at all | A comma after «Dear Gran» in a letter
test: «miss, 'Get out!' is an exclamation cos it's got !» What's the best reply? | It's a command: it tells someone to do something | Yes, any sentence with ! is an exclamation | It's a question, because it's short | It's a statement, because it tells a fact
test: ★★★ Which of these is an exclamation, a question, a command and a statement, in that order? | How fast she runs! / Does she run? / Run! / She runs. | Run! / How fast she runs! / She runs. / Does she run? | She runs. / Run! / Does she run? / How fast she runs! | How fast she runs! / Run! / Does she run? / She runs.
test: ★★★ «What time is it» and «What a time we had» both start with What. Which needs a question mark? | What time is it | What a time we had | Both of them | Neither of them
test: ★★★ «my nan lives in leeds and she has a cat called tom and he is fat.» How many capital letters are needed? | Three | Two | Four | Five
test: Year 1 kid: «i went home and i had tea and i went to bed and i was tired.» What's the most helpful advice? | Make I a capital and split it into shorter sentences | Add more «and»s so it flows better | Cross out every «i» | Write it all in capital letters
test: «shut the door, it's freezing». Which part is a command? | Shut the door | It's freezing | The comma | The whole thing is a question
test: In a cartoon, a character shouts «OH NO!!!!!!!!» in every speech bubble. What happens if a writer does that all the time? | The marks lose their effect | Readers get more excited each time | It becomes good grammar | Nothing, more is always better
test: What would happen if a long story had no full stops at all? | It would be very hard to read | It would be quicker and easier to read | Readers would never get tired | It would turn into a poem
test: «ugh it was raining, we stayed in». Which fix is right? | It was raining, so we stayed in. | It was raining, we stayed, in. | it was raining so we stayed in. | It was raining, we stayed in, and we watched a film.
test: Grandad says «Stop.» isn't a real sentence, «it's nowt but a word». (nowt is northern for nothing.) Who is right? | It's a real sentence, a command with «you» understood | Grandad, one word can never be a sentence | Neither, «Stop.» is a question, because you could ask it of somebody | Grandad, but only because it has no capital
test: Lucy: «Ey up! Fancy a brew?» What does «Ey up» mean? | Hello | Get up | Hurry up | Look up there
exam: Identify the type of sentence exemplified by «Close the door.» | A command | A statement | A question | An exclamation, the sentence expressing strong feeling
exam: Which of the following constitutes an exclamation as defined in the grammar of the primary curriculum? | What a remarkable view this is! | Kindly be seated! | The view is remarkable! | Is the view remarkable?
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sentence «It was raining, we went inside.» | It joins two sentences by a comma alone, and so requires correction | It is correctly punctuated | It lacks a verb | It is a command addressed to the persons who went inside
exam: Ascertain which of the following groups of words constitutes a complete sentence. | Stop. | The dog. | Under the old oak tree. | A big red bus with a broken window.
